   "detail": "Shared 50:50 with Saudi Arabia; offshore finds since 2024"
  }
 ],
 "notes_map": "Kuwait's fields fall into four areas. South and East Kuwait is dominated by Greater Burgan (Burgan, Magwa and Ahmadi), the world's second-largest oil field after Ghawar, covering roughly 800 square kilometres; in 2013 it still produced about half of Kuwait's oil (EIA, 2013). North Kuwait has Raudhatain and Sabriya, the deep Jurassic gas and light-oil fields served by the Jurassic Production Facilities, and Ratqa, where heavy oil is produced from the shallow Lower Fars sands with steam. West Kuwait has Minagish and Umm Gudair, which produce from the Minagish Oolite. The Partitioned Zone is shared 50:50 with Saudi Arabia: Wafra onshore and Khafji and Hout offshore, plus the Dorra gas field, which Iran also claims. Since 2024 KOC has announced offshore discoveries at Al-Nokhatha (about 3.2 billion barrels of oil equivalent) and Al-Jlaiaa. Click an area on the right to zoom in. Field positions were taken from the KOC fields map and the Horn giant-field database and are approximate.",
 "notes_regions": "In 1990 Greater Burgan supplied about two-thirds of KOC's output (Office of the Special Assistant for Gulf War Illnesses, 2000), and in 2013 it still produced about half of Kuwait's oil (EIA, 2013). North Kuwait holds Raudhatain and Sabriya and the deep Jurassic gas and light-oil play; West Kuwait holds Minagish and Umm Gudair. The Partitioned Zone, shared 50:50 with Saudi Arabia, contains Wafra onshore and Khafji and Hout offshore. Offshore discoveries since 2024 include Al-Nokhatha, about 3.2 billion barrels of oil equivalent (KUNA, 2024), and Al-Jlaiaa (Offshore Engineer, 2025). Click a card to zoom into an area.",
